  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM MDT Thu Apr 16 2026\n\nThe area will be under southwest flow today allowing for some breezy \nconditions. Late tonight and into tomorrow, the Sacramento Mtns are \nexpected to see some breezy conditions overnight. A Wind Advisory \nremains in effect for this area tonight with expected winds of 25-35 \nmph with gusts to 55 mph. Strongest winds looks to be around 6AM or \nso. Winds stay breezy up there in the afternoon but will be a little \nless than what we'll see tonight/tomorrow morning. Another breezy \nday tomorrow for the rest of the area. West winds of 15-25 mph with \ngusts to 40 mph expected during the afternoon. Blowing dust possible \nin normal dust areas, but uncertain how much visibilities will be \nreduced. The breezy days we had recently did not cause a lot of dust \nissues, but certainly some reductions in visibility. \n\nWinds start to relax after sunset on Saturday but will begin \nshifting northerly and then easterly just after midnight on Saturday \ndue to an incoming side door and backdoor cold front. This will \ncreate some breezy to windy conditions as the front pushes through. \nWinds start to relax heading into the afternoon hours with all sites \nwith an east wind. Breezes won't be too significant, but maybe 10-20 \nmph that afternoon. But another round of breezy to windy conditions \nexpected late Saturday morning and into Sunday as a secondary push \nof cool air comes in from the Plains. The windiest conditions will \nbe seen on west facing slopes (like west El Paso and the Hueco \nTanks). This will be true with the first push as well due to an \neasterly wind. In addition, we could see another appearance of hazy \nskies from both pushes from the front. Temperatures will run 8-15 \ndegrees below average on Sunday with highs in the upper 60s/low 70s \nin the lowlands and mid 40s to mid 50s in the area mountains. Rain \nchances continue to look possible as moisture seeps into the area \nallowing for some rain chances. Best shot right now looks like west \nTX with lesser chances elsewhere. Time frame will be Sunday \nafternoon and evening. Looking much breezier on Sunday afternoon \nwith a southeast wind at 15-25 mph sustained across the area. \nBlowing dust possible again on Sunday. An east wind causing blowing \ndust isn't super common but it can happen. Last year we had a strong \nbackdoor cold front that caused windy conditions everywhere, \nincluding the Lordsburg Playa. Blowing dust occurred on the Playa\nand caused extremely dangerous visibility that unfortunately \nended in a few deaths.",
